Ah—Indigo Hammer the word WAR, not merely the phenomenon. Yes. That yields more ore.

Take the word apart through multiple cuts:

\boxed{\text{WAR}}

Historical cut: English war comes through Old North French werre, related to Germanic forms carrying senses around confusion, strife, discord, mixture/turmoil. That’s already interesting: buried beneath the modern object “war” is something closer to a disturbed ordering of relations.

Now hammer it from other directions—not claiming these as etymology, but deliberately using the word as semantic material:

WAR \rightarrow W + AR

W = double-U. Two U’s. Two sides. Two valleys. Two opposing curves sharing one glyph.

U + U \rightarrow W

That gives a Pantheon reading:

War begins when two U’s become one W.

Two perspectives become coupled into one adversarial system.

Then:

WAR \rightarrow W + A + R

Read relationally:

W — We / Double-U
A — Against / At / Axis
R — Resistance / Relation / Return

So one synthetic rendering becomes:

\boxed{\text{WAR} = \text{We Against Resistance}}

But rotate the relations:

\boxed{\text{WAR} = \text{We Are Related}}

Same letters. Completely different control policy.

That’s an Indigo Hammer hit: the opponent and protagonist constitute the conflict together. Remove either pole and the relational object called “war” disappears.

And then your show of force comment gives another layer.

A show of force is fascinating precisely because its desired endpoint can be:

\text{display capability}
\rightarrow
\text{change prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{change decision}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{no force required}}

So:

\boxed{\text{Force demonstrated} \neq \text{Force expended}}

The demonstration operates primarily on the opponent’s model of the future.

That’s almost a counterfactual weapon: the consequential event is something that doesn’t happen because credible capability changed expectations beforehand.

Hammer again:

WAR → AWE

Only one letter changes.

And awe can be produced by overwhelming perceived scale/capability without collision.

So there’s a useful Pantheon transformation:

WAR
\xrightarrow{\text{credible signal}}
AWE
\xrightarrow{\text{updated prediction}}
DETERRENCE
\xrightarrow{}
NO\ WAR

Which suggests a broader primitive worth stealing:

\boxed{\textbf{Potential Force > Expended Force}}

when the receiver can reliably infer the potential.

That’s your suspense-as-potential-energy idea translated into strategic interaction: capability held in reserve can alter behavior precisely because everyone understands what could follow.

And there is an even prettier hammer strike:

war / ward / warn / wary

Not identical etymological mechanisms in every detail, but semantically they form a useful neighborhood:

\text{perceive threat}
\rightarrow \text{WARN}
\rightarrow \text{be WARY}
\rightarrow \text{WARD}
\rightarrow \text{avoid WAR}

The Indigo Hammer turns the word into its own little defensive control loop.

The highest show of force may therefore be the one powerful enough that it remains a show.